1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,055
In this video, I'm
going to discuss

2
00:00:02,055 --> 00:00:06,060
application containers and
Docker for embedded systems.

3
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:09,060
One understand the
difference between

4
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:11,250
containerization
and virtualization,

5
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:13,830
understand the Linux
kernel capabilities

6
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:15,660
that support containerization,

7
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:17,460
and understand how you can use

8
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:20,500
containers in embedded
development workflow.

9
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:24,195
What are application containers?

10
00:00:24,195 --> 00:00:27,770
Application containers are
really a packaging mechanism

11
00:00:27,770 --> 00:00:29,690
that abstracts applications from

12
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:32,440
whatever environment
they intend to run from.

13
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:35,160
It's a way that you can deploy

14
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:37,220
applications consistently,

15
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:39,350
regardless of how the
target environment

16
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,570
is set up specifically.

17
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:43,850
If I have a specific
application that I

18
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:45,665
want to be able to
deploy to, let's say,

19
00:00:45,665 --> 00:00:47,600
a server running in
the data center,

20
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:48,905
a server in the Cloud,

21
00:00:48,905 --> 00:00:51,815
a developer laptop, or
even an embedded device,

22
00:00:51,815 --> 00:00:53,420
and I want that
application to work the

23
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:56,130
same in any of those scenarios,

24
00:00:56,130 --> 00:00:57,680
and I don't want
to be dependent on

25
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,320
specific versions of
dependent applications,

26
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:02,450
I can put bundle all
the dependencies into

27
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:03,860
a container and run

28
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:05,750
that container on any
one of those targets,

29
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:09,070
and get the same
execution environment.

30
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:14,210
Containers allow developer
and IT teams to focus on

31
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:16,550
different parts that are unique

32
00:01:16,550 --> 00:01:20,610
to their role in the team.

33
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:23,060
For instance, a
developer needs to

34
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:25,325
focus on the application
logic and the dependency.

35
00:01:25,325 --> 00:01:29,870
Which specific version
of applications and

36
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:31,810
libraries need to
be installed in

37
00:01:31,810 --> 00:01:33,140
the roof file
system in order for

38
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:35,135
my application to work properly?

39
00:01:35,135 --> 00:01:36,770
Which versions have been

40
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:39,115
successfully tested
with my application.

41
00:01:39,115 --> 00:01:40,400
That should be the developer's

42
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:42,095
responsibility to know that,

43
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:45,140
to document that as a
part of their setup,

44
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:47,090
and to provide a way to

45
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:49,930
deliver that specific
tested instance.

46
00:01:49,930 --> 00:01:51,920
It allows then IT teams

47
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:53,930
to focus on the deployment
and management.

48
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,940
When should this application
be started and stopped?

49
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:58,715
How much memory or storage

50
00:01:58,715 --> 00:02:00,920
should the application
be allowed to use of

51
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:02,870
the overall memory and

52
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:05,040
storage that I've got on
my server, for instance?

53
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:07,910
Which ports should I allow it to

54
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:12,420
access or should be exposed
externally on my server?

55
00:02:13,490 --> 00:02:18,850
When we talk about
application containerization,

56
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:23,000
at first glance it sounds
a lot like virtualization.

57
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:28,120
If you are using
Ubuntu virtual machine

58
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:29,710
for your builds this semester,

59
00:02:29,710 --> 00:02:32,050
you're familiar with the
term virtualization,

60
00:02:32,050 --> 00:02:33,880
and you're familiar with

61
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:35,740
the concept of using
a hypervisor and

62
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:40,215
virtualization to virtualize
a guest operating system.

63
00:02:40,215 --> 00:02:42,445
A container is similar,

64
00:02:42,445 --> 00:02:44,155
but it's different in a key way,

65
00:02:44,155 --> 00:02:46,600
which is that virtualization,

66
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:48,610
you've got a whole hypervisor
layer where you're

67
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:51,605
simulating a new guest
operating system.

68
00:02:51,605 --> 00:02:55,680
With a container and an
application container,

69
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,820
you aren't including
the hypervisor layer.

70
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:01,340
You aren't simulating
a different kernel.

71
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:03,470
You're really using the
host operating system

72
00:03:03,470 --> 00:03:04,820
in the associated kernel

73
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:08,660
to run the applications
in dedicated containers.

74
00:03:08,660 --> 00:03:12,695
We're sharing the kernel
in the case of Linux,

75
00:03:12,695 --> 00:03:15,110
but really the host
operating system components

76
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:19,245
with the host system.

77
00:03:19,245 --> 00:03:21,635
Whereas in the
virtual machine case,

78
00:03:21,635 --> 00:03:23,210
we've got a hypervisor
that's creating

79
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:26,460
a completely virtualized
host system as well.

80
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:30,380
Some of the features that
are built into Linux kernel

81
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,380
that support containers
and that are

82
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:36,769
used to build a container
support mechanism

83
00:03:36,769 --> 00:03:39,035
, first is namespaces.

84
00:03:39,035 --> 00:03:41,720
A namespace allows you to
create isolated instance

85
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:44,780
of a resource using
the clone system call.

86
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:46,925
You can think of clone
as similar to fork,

87
00:03:46,925 --> 00:03:49,340
where we're taking a copy
of a process in memory

88
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:53,090
and making a separate copy of
it that we can manipulate.

89
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:57,140
Clone can work on separate
things other than processes,

90
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:59,120
though it can work
on networking,

91
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:01,790
it can work on specific ports,

92
00:04:01,790 --> 00:04:04,265
and it can work on mount
points for storage.

93
00:04:04,265 --> 00:04:07,520
It can also work on process IDs.

94
00:04:07,520 --> 00:04:09,680
Trees or process IDs in which

95
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:11,760
processes can interact
with each other.

96
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:14,285
That can all be controlled
based on namespaces.

97
00:04:14,285 --> 00:04:16,815
Second thing used
is control group.

98
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:20,045
Once we've defined
our namespaces,

99
00:04:20,045 --> 00:04:23,315
you can use control
groups to limit

100
00:04:23,315 --> 00:04:25,520
the constrained groups of

101
00:04:25,520 --> 00:04:27,760
processes that are
used in namespaces.

102
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,570
For instance, if I want to say

103
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:31,490
that I shouldn't have
more than x amount

104
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:34,280
of memory or CPU allocated

105
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:37,525
to a specific group
of processes.

106
00:04:37,525 --> 00:04:39,470
I can do that through the
control group's feature

107
00:04:39,470 --> 00:04:41,725
of the kernel.

108
00:04:41,725 --> 00:04:44,185
Then the third is union mounts

109
00:04:44,185 --> 00:04:46,030
is a way that we can combine

110
00:04:46,030 --> 00:04:47,965
multiple directories
into a single one

111
00:04:47,965 --> 00:04:49,570
that has the combined contents.

112
00:04:49,570 --> 00:04:50,980
This was covered in

113
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:54,260
the read-only file
system video series.

114
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,120
Talking about
Process Namespaces,

115
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,370
normally a process
tree would contain

116
00:04:59,370 --> 00:05:01,740
a reference to any process
that's running in the kernel.

117
00:05:01,740 --> 00:05:03,510
If you had a process
that's running

118
00:05:03,510 --> 00:05:05,370
with sufficient privileges and

119
00:05:05,370 --> 00:05:07,125
that process had a
reference the PID

120
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:09,795
of another process
in the namespace,

121
00:05:09,795 --> 00:05:14,940
it could kill the process send
signals to it, et cetera.

122
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:17,295
With a namespace,
you can introduce

123
00:05:17,295 --> 00:05:19,035
what's called a
nested process tree.

124
00:05:19,035 --> 00:05:23,820
Meaning that, a child
PID or a child process

125
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:26,100
would have access and could

126
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:29,055
view other processes
in its namespace,

127
00:05:29,055 --> 00:05:32,685
but it couldn't view the content
in the parent namespace.

128
00:05:32,685 --> 00:05:34,590
From a parent perspective,

129
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:35,820
it would be able to see

130
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:38,010
the child PIDs and
interact with them,

131
00:05:38,010 --> 00:05:39,330
but the child PIDs couldn't do

132
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:41,490
the opposite with
the parent PIDs.

133
00:05:41,490 --> 00:05:42,960
This is all supported with

134
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:45,180
the clone function that

135
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:48,100
I mentioned earlier
and CLONE_NEWPID.

136
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:52,095
In addition to processes,

137
00:05:52,095 --> 00:05:54,345
you can create
network namespaces

138
00:05:54,345 --> 00:05:55,920
where you can have
a different set of

139
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:58,485
network interfaces exposed to

140
00:05:58,485 --> 00:06:02,440
processes based on
namespaces that you define.

141
00:06:02,440 --> 00:06:05,840
Network ports that
are defined on

142
00:06:05,840 --> 00:06:07,940
a parent process might not be

143
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:10,810
accessible on a child
process, for instance.

144
00:06:10,810 --> 00:06:13,230
You can do this so that
you have different loop

145
00:06:13,230 --> 00:06:15,630
back interfaces for networks

146
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:18,000
based on whether your
processes running

147
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,565
in a parent or a
child namespace.

148
00:06:20,565 --> 00:06:24,509
Then you can create routing
between those networks,

149
00:06:24,509 --> 00:06:29,985
again using these network
namespace functions.

150
00:06:29,985 --> 00:06:31,950
CLONE_NEWNET is the thing

151
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:35,070
that supports this functionality

152
00:06:35,070 --> 00:06:37,440
through the clone command.

153
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:40,590
What you can see with
a network namespace

154
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:42,330
is a completely
different view of

155
00:06:42,330 --> 00:06:46,815
the network availability
on the host.

156
00:06:46,815 --> 00:06:49,935
For instance, if I create
a new network namespace,

157
00:06:49,935 --> 00:06:52,320
I might see only the
loopback port address

158
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:54,570
on the host because I
haven't specifically

159
00:06:54,570 --> 00:06:56,790
exposed the Ethernet port that's

160
00:06:56,790 --> 00:06:59,610
available in the
parent PID namespace.

161
00:06:59,610 --> 00:07:01,785
This allows us to
specifically control

162
00:07:01,785 --> 00:07:04,320
how a process is able
to see the network

163
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,900
and what type of access
it would be able

164
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:10,540
to have to the host
network resources.

165
00:07:10,670 --> 00:07:14,460
Then control groups will
organize a process into

166
00:07:14,460 --> 00:07:15,780
groups and then be able to

167
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:18,090
limit resources for that group.

168
00:07:18,090 --> 00:07:20,190
It can control things
like the amount of

169
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:22,515
CPU time, the memory available.

170
00:07:22,515 --> 00:07:25,050
It can also freeze
and resume execution

171
00:07:25,050 --> 00:07:28,690
of processes running
in a specific group.

172
00:07:29,390 --> 00:07:31,680
Then union mounts,

173
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:35,490
this is again a
generic reference to

174
00:07:35,490 --> 00:07:37,755
the content that we covered in

175
00:07:37,755 --> 00:07:41,295
the read-only UnionFS video.

176
00:07:41,295 --> 00:07:43,020
Union mounts were started in

177
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:45,435
the early '90s to
spot writable CDs,

178
00:07:45,435 --> 00:07:47,910
really with the idea of

179
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:51,360
merging read-only file system
content with read-write

180
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:53,115
file system content to get

181
00:07:53,115 --> 00:07:55,080
the equivalent of

182
00:07:55,080 --> 00:07:58,350
the two different
merged directory trees.

183
00:07:58,350 --> 00:08:00,810
There have been three
implementation sense

184
00:08:00,810 --> 00:08:04,170
originally coming up with
the idea of union mounts.

185
00:08:04,170 --> 00:08:06,870
UnionFS and aufs
were two of them.

186
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:08,910
Then OverlayFS is
the first version

187
00:08:08,910 --> 00:08:11,490
that's been accepted in
the main line kernel.

188
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:13,500
Again, that's discussed in

189
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:16,650
a little more detail in the
read-only file system video.

190
00:08:16,650 --> 00:08:18,510
But the basic idea is to

191
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:20,670
allow a read/write
overlay on top of

192
00:08:20,670 --> 00:08:24,600
a read-only overlay and be
able to conditionally put

193
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:26,415
parts of the overlay in RAM

194
00:08:26,415 --> 00:08:29,770
instead of persistent storage.

195
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:33,690
Docker is an open
platform that can be

196
00:08:33,690 --> 00:08:36,735
used to create and run
containerized applications.

197
00:08:36,735 --> 00:08:40,019
It's based on a Docker engine
client-server application,

198
00:08:40,019 --> 00:08:42,675
where there's a docker
daemon that can start,

199
00:08:42,675 --> 00:08:44,624
