200
00:08:44,624 --> 00:08:45,780
and there's a client that can

201
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:49,215
interact with the daemon
to perform commands.

202
00:08:49,215 --> 00:08:51,390
There's also optionally
a registry that

203
00:08:51,390 --> 00:08:53,384
can be used to
store docker images

204
00:08:53,384 --> 00:08:59,100
and share docker images or use
them on different systems.

205
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:00,930
Look at the way this interaction

206
00:09:00,930 --> 00:09:02,835
works from the docker client,

207
00:09:02,835 --> 00:09:04,815
you can use build to build

208
00:09:04,815 --> 00:09:07,395
a local container based
on a set of instructions.

209
00:09:07,395 --> 00:09:10,860
You can use pull to pull
containers from a registry,

210
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:15,240
and you can use run to run
local copies of containers.

211
00:09:15,240 --> 00:09:17,145
The docker daemon
is the one that's

212
00:09:17,145 --> 00:09:19,170
organizing which
containers are pulled

213
00:09:19,170 --> 00:09:21,810
locally and being able

214
00:09:21,810 --> 00:09:24,825
to start and stop them
based on commands.

215
00:09:24,825 --> 00:09:27,015
Then the registry
is where you can

216
00:09:27,015 --> 00:09:29,940
share and store
common applications.

217
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:33,570
For instance, this register
entry for NGINX is

218
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:37,395
a NGINX server,

219
00:09:37,395 --> 00:09:39,435
a web server that's

220
00:09:39,435 --> 00:09:42,015
free-running in a container
and all configured

221
00:09:42,015 --> 00:09:45,240
based on the
applications that NGINX

222
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:49,140
needs in order to support
running on it a container.

223
00:09:49,140 --> 00:09:52,130
Similarly, there's
an Ubuntu image in

224
00:09:52,130 --> 00:09:55,385
a registry and so rather than
having to install Ubuntu,

225
00:09:55,385 --> 00:09:58,790
you can just reference
the registry container

226
00:09:58,790 --> 00:10:03,150
and pull that in as the
basis of your docker image.

227
00:10:04,190 --> 00:10:06,810
There's some setup
steps you can do,

228
00:10:06,810 --> 00:10:10,080
to set up Docker on
your Ubuntu host and

229
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:11,580
there's links here to

230
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:14,280
the Docker CE instructions
to set this up,

